BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Probing New Physics through Higgs–Vector-Boson Couplings with th
 e ATLAS Detector
DTSTART:20261020T193000Z
DTEND:20261020T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T032900Z
UID:indico-event-10676@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Jiayi Chen (Simon Fraser University)\n\nThirteen yea
 rs after the discovery of the Higgs boson\, the LHC program has entered a 
 precision era in exploring this remarkable particle and its interactions. 
 Among them\, the Higgs–vector-boson coupling offers one of the cleanest 
 and most powerful windows onto physics beyond the Standard Model. Tiny dev
 iations in this interaction could reveal new phenomena\, including charge
 –parity (CP) violation in the Higgs sector. Such CP violation is not onl
 y a compelling target for collider measurements\, but also an essential in
 gredient in our broader quest to understand the matter–antimatter asymme
 try of the Universe.\nIn this seminar\, I will introduce how Effective Fie
 ld Theory provides a systematic framework for searching for CP-violating H
 iggs interactions. I will then discuss recent experimental and theoretical
  developments that have sharpened the precision of Higgs measurements\, wi
 th a focus on analyses using the full Run-2 proton-proton collision datase
 t collected by the ATLAS detector at a center-of-mass energy of 13 TeV.\n
  \nhttps://stanford.zoom.us/j/98973156241?pwd=cEU5RFdlVXoyc0JTeTlDMkozKzQ
 5UT09\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10676/
LOCATION:Stanford
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10676/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Telling events apart in CRESST: singles\, surface events and machi
 ne learning
DTSTART:20261022T193000Z
DTEND:20261022T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T032900Z
UID:indico-event-10806@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Praveen Murali (Heidelberg University)\n\nCRESST sea
 rches for sub-GeV dark matter with cryogeniccalorimeters read out by trans
 ition edge sensors\, reaching thresholdsbelow 10 eV. Its sensitivity is li
 mited by a low-energy excess and byanomalous events seen by only one of tw
 o sensors. I will present mywork on double-TES detectors: optimum-filter r
 econstruction\, taggingof anomalous singles and surface/bulk classificatio
 n of ⁵⁵Fe events. Iwill also show machine-learning methods for artifac
 t tagging\, whichwill matter for the planned upgrade to 200+ modules.\n \
 nhttps://stanford.zoom.us/j/98973156241?pwd=cEU5RFdlVXoyc0JTeTlDMkozKzQ5UT
 09\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10806/
