BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Progress toward a sterile neutrino search with optically levitated
  nanospheres
DTSTART:20261110T203000Z
DTEND:20261110T213000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T032900Z
UID:indico-event-10804@indico.slac.stanford.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Clarke Hardy (Yale University)\n\nThe origin and nat
 ure of neutrino masses remain key open questions in particle physics. With
  a simple extension to the Standard Model\, light neutrino masses can aris
 e via the seesaw mechanism\, in which each mass state has a heavy right-ha
 nded counterpart. In some models\, these right-handed “sterile” states
  fall in the keV to MeV mass range. The Quantum Invisible Particle Sensor 
 (QuIPS) collaboration is developing a search for sterile neutrinos utilizi
 ng silica nanospheres trapped at the focus of a laser in high vacuum. Due 
 to their extreme isolation from the environment\, such levitated optomecha
 nical systems have opened up new pathways for testing fundamental physics 
 with quantum-limited sensitivity. If the nanospheres are doped with beta o
 r electron capture isotopes\, detection of the recoil impulse accompanying
  the emission of decay products enables an event-by-event measurement of t
 he total momentum carried away\, including by otherwise invisible particle
 s. The Yale QuIPS team has already demonstrated impulse sensitivity of bet
 ter than 50 keV/c\, sufficient to resolve collisions of individual gas ato
 ms with a nanosphere and well below the momentum carried by neutrinos in t
 ypical decays. In this talk I will present an overview of the experiment\,
  showcase recent progress\, and discuss prospects for a first sterile neut
 rino search.\n \nhttps://stanford.zoom.us/j/98973156241?pwd=cEU5RFdlVXoyc
 0JTeTlDMkozKzQ5UT09\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10804/
URL:https://indico.slac.stanford.edu/event/10804/
END:VEVENT
